title Every tool in the room its name says
prompt Keaton: take all 26, bring the fleet down.
think The fleet came down first and in the right order -- the watcher before the ships, or it would have re-armed each one I stopped. The sentinel plus an ANCHORED pattern, never the bare filename that cost two ships an hour ago.
think Six ships held interrupted work. I left every one alone: one writer per checkout, and their own round-open stashes a dead lap's leavings into the dead-letter box on relaunch. Nothing of theirs is mine to commit.
think 26 became 34 once I asked the same question of the FIXTURE letter rooms, which follow the same rule and had eight of the same fault. One rule, one sweep.
think Two directories move as directories rather than as files. tools/l/mind-bin holds a shim that MUST be named git, because a PATH directory finds a program by its name; the room follows the directory's name and the file keeps its own. The census still reports that one as a mismatch, and it is right to -- it reads leaf basenames -- so it is named here rather than silenced.
obs A LITERAL SWEEP CANNOT SEE A PATH THAT IS BUILT AT RUNTIME. Three sites held tools/l/${seat}_seat_prompt.txt and one held a glob, so my 58-file replace passed over every one and fleet_roster went red on a control the move had quietly broken. The repair is better than the reference: the room is DERIVED from the seat's first letter -- cut -c1 -- which is the fold rule written as code, so a seat added tomorrow arrives findable without anyone editing a path.
obs THE PROMPTS NO LONGER SHARE A ROOM AND THAT IS THE POINT. Eleven seats, eleven rooms: bakery in b, copal in c, the three p-ships together. A glob over tools/?/ finds them all, and tool_path_resolve reaches any one of them from its basename alone -- which it could not do while they hid under l.
obs THE MOVE CAUGHT TWO SHELVES THE TREE READS AS LIVING. Two REDS shelves link to files that moved, and a shelf carries no one-clock stamp in its basename -- so by the tree's own test they are living, and readme_reach gated them. Repointed rather than resolved: a link is a promise, and moving a file is exactly when a promise comes due.
obs FOUR GUARDS FOUND WHAT THE SWEEP COULD NOT, AND EACH FOUND A DIFFERENT KIND OF INVISIBLE REFERENCE. declared_ceiling held an EXEMPTION naming the old path, so a file it had always excused was suddenly counted. living_card_ascii resolved its awk helper as a dirname-$0 SIBLING, and the helper had moved to its own room. fleet_round_open control COPIED the stash scan from a sibling path into a pen, so the pen silently held no scan and two cases failed on an absence. None is a link; none is a literal a grep would have shown me.
obs I NEARLY FALSIFIED A BYTE PIN AND THE CEILING GUARD IS WHAT CAUGHT IT. The sweep rewrote one line inside tools/c/chatgpt-mind.sh -- its own Usage: string naming its old path -- and that file is pinned BY SHA-256 in its peer adaptation note, ee508804. Restored byte for byte rather than restamped: a path is a place, a provenance witness is its bytes. Its Usage line still prints the elder path, which is the honest cost of a byte pin and cheaper than a false hash; the note now says so.
obs A STASH ROUND-TRIP UN-STAGED THREE DELETIONS AND THE INDEX LIED TO A GUARD. git ls-files reads the INDEX, so the ascii scan was handed three paths no longer on disk and refused as instrument=failed -- correctly. The repair was git add -A; the lesson is that a stash pop mid-move leaves the index a different shape than the tree, and every index-reading guard sees the difference before I do.
loom move letter_room_files=2030 misfiled_before=26 fixture_misfiled=8 moved=34 dirs=2
loom sweep living_citers=73 computed_paths_missed=4 caught_by=fleet_roster_control
loom fascia documents_reached=1795 broken_in_living=0 seed_ratchet=848 ceiling=848 tracked_links=ok
loom guards fleet_roster=GREEN fleet_watch=GREEN fleet_lap_verdict=GREEN fleet_rearm=GREEN birth_a_clone=GREEN exec_bit=GREEN tool_path=GREEN
file tools/f/fleet-loop.sh and fleet_lap.sh -- the prompt room derived from the seat's first letter
file tools/fixtures/f/fleet_roster_control.sh the glob widened to every letter room; two computed paths derived
file construction/CHECKPOINTS.md the cairn before the move, naming what every elder path resolves through
scope 34 files into the rooms their names say, 73 living citers repointed, four computed paths derived rather than replaced
status GREEN -- seven fleet guards, exec_bit, tool_path, tracked_link, readme_reach at zero, sow witness green
recommend keep-going -- the fleet comes back up bare in this same round, which is the half of a fleet-down window that must not be forgotten.
